Article 1º O The public sector shall publish in active transparency the information related to the investigation, instruction, and criminal trial, based on the right to access information provided in item XXXIII of article 5, item II of section 3 of article 37, and section 2 of article 216 of the Federal Constitution, for the consolidation of the National Homicide Clarification Indicator. Art. 2° It is the duty of public bodies and entities, especially those responsible for investigation,
instruction, and criminal judgment, to promote, independently of requests, the disclosure, on their
websites, of data and information related to the clarification of intentional homicide and other intentional
violent lethal crimes, in an open format and in accordance with §3 of art. 8 of Law No. 12,527 of 2011,
the Access to Information Law. Article 3. In disseminating the information referred to in Article 2, at a minimum, the following data produced and related to the performance of:

I – the judicial and technical-scientific police in each federal unit, annually:

a) the percentage of police inquiries related to intentional lethal violent crimes completed reported with authorship compared to the total of inquiries initiated, disaggregated by criminal type;

b) the number of police stations with exclusive attribution for investigating completed and attempted homicides and their location;

c) the number of criminalistics and papilology laboratories dedicated and exclusive to criminal investigation and their location;

d) the number of police officers assigned to specialized homicide investigation stations, by career, and their proportion per 100,000 inhabitants;

e) the number of criminal experts, papilologists, and medical examiners and their staffing and proportion per 100,000 inhabitants;

f) the stock of police inquiries for intentional lethal violent crimes per 100,000 inhabitants;

g) the average duration of police investigation of intentional lethal violent crimes, disaggregated by criminal type. II – from the Public Ministry in each federal unit:
a) percentage of occurrences of intentional lethal violent crimes that result in indictments

(a) criminal statistics compared to the total number of incidents reported for each specific year;
(b) the number of state prosecutors working in jury courts and their ratio per 100,000 inhabitants;

III – of the Judiciary in each federal unit:
(a) the percentage of cases involving homicide and other intentional lethal violent crimes that have been concluded out of the total number of cases with received indictments;
(b) the number of instruction hearings for homicide cases;
(c) the number of jury courts established for homicide cases;
(d) the backlog of open cases for completed intentional lethal violent crimes, identified separately;
(e) the number of active jury courts;
(f) the number of judges allocated to jury courts and their ratio per 100,000 inhabitants;

§1. In cities with over 100,000 inhabitants, the data provided in the preceding paragraph should be accompanied by geolocalization to identify the locations of intentional lethal violent crimes, both completed and attempted, as well as the locations of the bodies responsible for investigating and trying criminal cases resulting from these crimes.

§2. When disseminating data on intentional lethal violent crimes, the age, race, and gender of the victim, the investigator, and the defendant in the case should be specified. Article 4. The disclosure of the data and information provided in Article 3 shall occur in an aggregated and fragmented manner, and it shall be the responsibility of the governing bodies of the Judicial and Technical-Scientific Police, the Public Ministry, and the Judiciary of each federal unit to ensure this occurs at the end of each semester. Art. 5 The Union will standardize the format of information related to the criminal investigation phase. Art. 6. The format of the information to be disclosed by the Public Ministry and the Judiciary Power shall be standardized by their respective Councils to which they are linked. Article 7. In the disclosure of data and information referred to in this Law, those involved in the investigation or process shall not be personally identified. Article 8. The Union shall disseminate the National Homicide Clarification Indicator, composed of consolidated data in each federation entity and demonstrating the evolution of complaints filed and judgments rendered for occurrences of homicide and intentional lethal violent crimes, separated by the year in which the respective crimes were committed. Art. 9º The provisions of Article 32 of the Law on Access to Information apply, as appropriate, to actions carried out within the scope of this Law. Article 10. This Law enters into force on the date of its publication.

